nuisance.  The internal diameter of the chute shall be at least 300 mm.
In individual chute system, the inlet hopper shall be located in the passage near the kitchen and in the common chute system towards the end of the common passage.  Natural ventilation should be adequate to prevent any possible odour nuisance. There should be adequate lighting at this location.  For ground floor (floor 1), the inlet hoppers may be placed at a higher level, but should be easily accessible.  The access to the refuse chute shall be provided from well ventilated and well illuminated common corridor or lobby and preferably it should not be located opposite or adjacent to entry of individual flats or lift.
4.3.2 Opening for Feeding of Refuse Chute
Opening, with top or bottom hinged shutters with appropriate lockable latch, shall be provided for convenient accessing of the refuse chute by users.  It facilitates total building garbage collection at one single point and provides separate collection for dry and wet garbage.  The opening of the refuse chute may have separate receptacles for both wet and dry waste.  Mixed waste disposal may not be allowed.  For wet waste, there should be a provision for collection of leachate.  Chute system shall have the following features:
a) Sanitation system for internal cleaning of chutes, with sprinklers at every floor intake level, with disinfectant cleaning, which may preferably be automatic in operation;
b) Exhaust system with 25 to 35 air changes per hour;
c) Discharge end fire resistant door;
d) Chute may be of masonry, R.C.C pipe, asbestos cement pipe or suitable non- corrosive material, preferably stainless steel.  It should be rigid with smooth internal finish, high ductility and alkali/acid resistant properties {see also good practice [E-3(3)]};
e) For fire rating, reference should be made to relevant clauses of Part F ‘Fire and Life Safety’ of this NBCS;
f) Fire sprinklers shall be provided inside chute;
g) Vent shall be installed at roof level to remove foul smell and gases generated;
h) Chute should discharge to trolley directly;
j) Necessary automation of the system may be planned to avoid malfunction by manual operation; and k) All care shall be taken for ensuring accessibility to the chute for persons with disabilities.  The opening of the chute shall not be higher than 700 mm and there shall be a clear space of minimum 1 500 mm × 1 500 mm in front of the chute opening to gain easy access even for persons using wheelchairs.  There may be high colour contrast provided around the chute opening to make it easy to identify it.  The flooring in front of the chute opening may be texturally highly different from the rest of the floor.
4.3.3 Refuse Collection Chamber
The collection chamber may be located in ground floor or basement level, provided appropriate arrangement is made for drainage of the collection pit by gravity flow to ensure its dryness, an appropriate ramp access is provided for convenient removal of garbage from the collection pit, and satisfactory ventilation is provided for escape of gas and odour.
The floor of the chamber shall be provided with drainage through a 100 mm diameter trap and screen to prevent any solid matters flowing into the drain and the drain shall be connected to the sewer line.  The floor shall be finished with smooth hard surface for convenient cleaning.
The height of the collection chamber and vertical clearance under the bottom level of garbage chute shall be such that the garbage trolley can be conveniently placed.
The collection chamber shall be provided with appropriate shutter to prevent access of scavenging animals like cattle, dogs, cats and rats.
Building owners and associations should be encouraged about proper usage of this system which otherwise can be clogged and malfunction.  They may also be encouraged to have a single agent/person for door to door collection having effective segregation at source and ensuring hygienic conditions and authorizing only such NATIONAL BUILDING CONSTRUCTION STANDARDS 2026

