National Building Code Sectional Committee, CED 46 FOREWORD This NBCS (Part E/Section 3) covers the solid waste management systems, assessment of per solid waste quantities and treatment of solid waste within the building, building complexes and their built environments.
In the first version of the Code formulated in 1970, as part of the National Building Code of India, three separate sections of this Part on ‘Plumbing Services’, were brought out, namely, Section 1 Water Supply, Section 2 Drainage and Sanitation, and Section 3 Gas Supply.  These sections were subsequently revised in 1983 and in 2005.
In the second revision in 2005, the Part 9 was renamed as ‘Plumbing Services (Including solid waste management)’ and provisions on solid waste management were included for the first time under Section 1 which contained in it, Water supply, drainage and sanitation including solid waste management.  Gas Supply was covered in Section 2 of Part 9 of the Code.
In the third revision in 2016, to comprehensively address the various and distinct features related to the plumbing aspects, this Part 9 was rearranged as follows:
Section 1 Water supply Section 2 Drainage and sanitation Section 3 Solid waste management Section 4 Gas supply The significant changes included in the 2016 version of NBC included: Elaborative provisions on solid waste management systems; inclusions of the provisions for persons with disabilities towards operation of refuse chutes;
recommendation of electrically operated dumb waiters for 5 storied (and above); inclusion of assessment of per capita waste quantity; referencing the latest statutory rules and regulations; inclusion of detailed provisions on treatment methods; inclusion of annexes namely Annex A on municipal solid waste generation, Annex B on treatment of food wastes; and review of terminology.
In this revision, this publication has been renamed as National Building Construction Standards (NBCS).
The significant changes incorporated in this revision include:
a) References to the latest rules and regulations have been made;
b) Terminology clause has been reviewed and updated;
c) Circularity aspects of solid waste management has been included;
d) Domestic waste now covers all the remaining types of wastes generated;
e) Caution with respect to plastic waste by avoiding their down cycling is included;
f) Primary segregation and secondary segregation have been detailed in sorting at source;
g) Additional options towards efficient use of refuse chutes are included;
h) Treatment options namely aerobic processes, vermi-composting have been updated taking care of the best practices in these activities;
j) Provisions for the colour coding of waste bins have been incorporated to ensure proper segregation of solid waste;
k) Provisions have been incorporated for the treatment of biodegradable waste by household and commercial facilities; and m) References to all the concerned Indian Standards have been updated.
